// In-depth explanations, weeks 1-4. Format: [question text prefix, deep explanation, [note for each option in written order]]
DEEP.push(
  // ---------- WEEK 1 ----------
  ["How is machine learning (ML) related",
   "AI is the broad goal: machines showing intelligent behaviour. It includes hand-written expert systems (GOFAI), search, planning, and learning. ML is the sub-field where the behaviour is not programmed rule by rule but fitted from examples. The model has free parameters (weights, thresholds) and an optimiser adjusts them to minimise an error on data. Deep learning is in turn a subset of ML that uses multi-layer neural networks. Remember the nesting: AI ⊃ ML ⊃ DL.",
   ["Correct: ML is the data-driven subset of AI.",
    "Reversed. ML sits inside AI, and robotics is only one application.",
    "They are tightly related: ML is today the dominant way of building AI.",
    "ML is a family of algorithms, not hardware. GPUs are the hardware that made it practical."]],
  ["Which of the following is a classification task",
   "The question to ask is: is the output a category or a quantity? Classification maps an input to one of a finite set of labels (pneumonia / no pneumonia). The model usually outputs a score or probability per class, and we pick the highest. Regression maps to a continuous value on a number line. The same input (an X-ray) could be used for either, depending on what we predict: 'pneumonia yes/no' is classification, 'lung opacity percentage' is regression.",
   ["Correct: two discrete outcomes, so binary classification.",
    "Krill density is a continuous number, so regression.",
    "A dollar price is continuous, so regression.",
    "Temperature in degrees is continuous, so regression."]],
  ["Which of the following is a regression task",
   "Regression predicts a real-valued quantity, and we measure error by how far the prediction is (e.g. squared error), not just right/wrong. Heart rate in bpm can be 71.4 or 72.0, and being off by 1 bpm is better than being off by 20. That notion of distance is why regression uses losses like MSE. Classification tasks use losses like cross-entropy over classes.",
   ["Correct: beats per minute is a continuous quantity.",
    "Approve/deny is a two-class decision, so classification.",
    "Choosing 1 of 1000 classes is multi-class classification.",
    "Spam/not spam is binary classification."]],
  ["What is the main difference between classic AI",
   "Symbolism (GOFAI) assumes intelligence is manipulation of explicit symbols and rules, which a human expert must write down: IF income < X THEN deny, or a physics equation with a few fitted constants. Connectionism (neural networks) assumes knowledge is distributed in many numeric weights learned from data. GOFAI was limited by the expert's ability to formalise knowledge. Connectionism was held back for decades by slow computers and lack of data, until PCs, the Internet (data) and GPUs arrived.",
   ["Correct: humans write the rules in GOFAI, while the machine learns the parameters in ML.",
    "Reversed. Neural networks are the connectionist (modern) approach.",
    "Reversed. Data-hungry is the ML side; GOFAI needs expertise rather than data.",
    "They are historically and conceptually opposite approaches."]],
  ["Which statement correctly contrasts supervised",
   "Supervised learning learns a mapping x → y from pairs where the 'teacher' signal y is given (labels, measured values). Unsupervised learning has only x and must discover structure: clusters (k-means, GMM), low-dimensional manifolds (PCA, autoencoders), or densities. Supervised learning is usually more accurate for a fixed task but depends on costly, sometimes noisy labels; labelling is a whole industry. Unsupervised and self-supervised approaches exploit the huge amount of unlabelled data.",
   ["Correct: labels are the key difference.",
    "Both need data, and supervised is the one that needs labels.",
    "Not true. They solve different problems, and accuracy depends on the task.",
    "Supervised covers both classification and regression; unsupervised covers clustering, density estimation and more."]],
  ["Where is data bias coming from",
   "Bias means the model is systematically wrong for some group or situation. It can enter at every stage of the data pipeline. (1) Class imbalance: rare classes get little weight, so the model favours the majority. (2) Sampling: if data is collected only from one hospital, population or camera, the training distribution differs from reality (e.g. only healthy white volunteers). (3) Labelling: annotators bring their own subjectivity, errors and cultural assumptions. Because all three are real, the 'all of the above' option is right.",
   ["Partially true but not the only source.",
    "Partially true but not the only source.",
    "Partially true but not the only source.",
    "Correct: imbalance, sampling and human labelling can all introduce bias."]],
  ["'Thinking, Fast and Slow'",
   "Kahneman's System 1 is fast, automatic, unconscious and able to run several processes at once (driving while talking). It handles known problems through learned skills. System 2 is slow, effortful, conscious reasoning that handles one thing at a time (solving 17 × 24). The lecture compares current ML to System 1: with enough data it learns skills, but we don't know how to make machines do System 2 style reasoning on genuinely new problems.",
   ["Wrong: System 1 is the fast one.",
    "Wrong: System 2 is the logical, reasoning one.",
    "Distractor. 'Emotional' is not the property the lecture uses; the key properties are fast, automatic and multitask.",
    "Correct: System 1 runs automatically and can multitask."]],
  ["According to the lecture, which describes Brain System 2",
   "The lecture's comparison table has three columns. A traditional computer has exact, vast memory, is fast and reliable, but only solves problems coded by humans. System 1 handles automated unconscious actions and known problems, needs learning, and is relatively fast and multitask. System 2 is conscious thinking that solves unknown problems by reasoning, but is relatively slow and does one task at a time. Being able to place each property in the right column is a typical exam check.",
   ["Correct: this is the System 2 column of the slide.",
    "This describes System 1.",
    "This describes a traditional computer.",
    "This describes System 1."]],
  ["An AI system works well at deployment",
   "Data drift (or dataset shift) means the statistical distribution of inputs changes after deployment. Here the new camera changes colours, sharpness and noise, so images map to different locations in the learned feature space, and the decision boundaries no longer fit. The model itself hasn't changed; the world has. Mitigations include monitoring input statistics, recalibrating or retraining on new data, and data augmentation that covers equipment variation.",
   ["Correct: the input distribution changed over time.",
    "Bias is systematic error for a subgroup present from the start, not a change over time.",
    "Overfitting to the test set comes from repeatedly tuning on test data, not hardware change.",
    "Underfitting would have shown poor performance from the start."]],
  ["A new patient's scan",
   "Supervised models interpolate within the region of input space covered by training data. A case unlike anything in training lands in an unexplored region, where the decision boundary is essentially arbitrary, so the prediction is unreliable. The model often doesn't signal uncertainty, which makes this dangerous. The lecture calls it 'unknown data' or lack of generalisation. Remedies: more diverse training data, out-of-distribution detection, and calibrated uncertainty.",
   ["Correct: the case was not represented in the training data.",
    "Drift needs a change over time (e.g. new equipment); here the case was simply never covered.",
    "Label noise means wrong training labels, which isn't described here.",
    "Vanishing gradients are a training optimisation problem, not a deployment failure."]],
  ["A cancer model was trained",
   "This is the lecture's bias example. The training set lacked black patients, so the model never learned where their scans should map. Their cancer scans end up near the healthy cluster and are missed: higher false negatives for one subgroup. Overall accuracy can still look high, which is why fairness evaluation must report performance per subgroup. It's a sampling bias problem, fixed by representative data collection and subgroup analysis.",
   ["Correct: systematic error for an under-represented subgroup.",
    "Nothing changed over time, so it's not drift.",
    "No one deliberately perturbed the inputs.",
    "No one poisoned the training data with a trigger."]],
  ["In the lecture's 'machine learning = creating maps'",
   "A phone image is 4000 × 3000 × 3 = 36 million numbers. A network's layers of multiplications and additions transform those numbers into a short vector: a location on a learned 'map' (latent space). Similar images land near each other, so a new image can be classified by where it lands. Generative AI goes the other way: pick a location and decode it into data. That location vector is the embedding. 'Latent' means it's a hidden variable not directly observed.",
   ["Correct: an embedding is the learned location (vector) on the map.",
    "Pixels are the raw input before mapping.",
    "Labels are the supervision targets, not the internal representation.",
    "The learning rate is an optimiser hyperparameter."]],
  ["In the 'map' view of ML, how does a chatbot",
   "An LLM maps the text so far (as tokens) to a probability distribution over the next token. It samples one, appends it, and repeats: autoregressive generation. In map terms it's moving step by step along a trajectory in embedding space. It doesn't look up stored answers. Everything is generated from learned weights, which is also why it can hallucinate plausible but false text.",
   ["Correct: next-token prediction, repeated.",
    "LLMs do not retrieve whole stored answers (unless augmented with retrieval tools).",
    "Hand-written grammar rules are GOFAI, not LLMs.",
    "k-means is a clustering algorithm unrelated to text generation."]],
  ["What triggered the 2012",
   "In 2012 AlexNet, a deep CNN trained on GPUs, won the ImageNet challenge by a large margin over hand-engineered feature pipelines. The lecture links this to the 1872 shift in painting (impressionism): move from building explicit models to letting data speak. The enablers were (1) generic models such as neural networks, (2) huge labelled datasets (ImageNet: 14M images, 20k+ categories), and (3) cheap parallel compute (GPUs).",
   ["Correct: deep CNNs plus ImageNet plus GPUs.",
    "Decision trees were decades older and didn't cause this shift.",
    "Symbolic logic programming is the GOFAI era (1970s-80s).",
    "Chess (Deep Blue, 1997) used search, not data-driven deep learning."]],
  ["Which statement best distinguishes AGI",
   "Today's systems are mostly narrow AI: very good at specific tasks. AGI would match humans across the full range of cognitive tasks, including learning new skills on its own. ASI would exceed the best humans in essentially every domain. The lecture's open questions: can AGI be reached by scaling current models? Is today's AI 'nowhere near super intelligence'? And what ethical issues arise if ASI does not share human goals?",
   ["Correct: general human-level vs beyond-human.",
    "Nonsense definitions, since 'general' means broad, not one task.",
    "They are concepts, not benchmarks.",
    "Reversed: ASI is beyond AGI, not weaker."]],
  ["Which point did the lecture raise",
   "The lecture questions whether scaling data and compute is the same as intelligence. A human brain runs on ~20 W and can learn a new skill from a handful of examples; large models need enormous datasets and megawatts. True intelligence would also mean learning new skills autonomously and solving never-seen problems. Current AI is still very useful, and it will change how we work, even if it isn't intelligent in that full sense.",
   ["Correct: the energy and data-efficiency argument.",
    "Wrong: the lecture stresses current AI can do a lot.",
    "Memory is not the limitation discussed.",
    "Wrong: deep models are notoriously hard to explain."]],
  ["Consider the statements:\nA/ System 1",
   "Check each statement. A: System 1 is automated and unconscious, so true. B: 'relatively fast and multitask' describes System 1, not System 2, so false. C: System 2 is conscious reasoning that handles unknown problems, so true. D: System 1 does require learning (skills become automatic through practice), so false. That leaves A and C. In combination questions, mark each statement T/F first, then match the option.",
   ["Correct: A and C are the true statements.",
    "B is false (it describes System 1).",
    "D is false (System 1 skills are learned).",
    "Both B and D are false."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 2 ----------
  ["What is the core assumption made when performing supervised",
   "Supervised learning assumes y = f(x) + noise for some unknown function f, and that the training pairs are samples of that same relationship that will hold for future data. The model is our hypothesis for f, and training tunes its parameters so predictions match the examples. If no relationship exists, or it changes at deployment, no amount of fitting helps. 'All models are wrong, but some are useful' (George Box).",
   ["Correct: we assume a learnable input-output relationship.",
    "If the data had no structure, there would be nothing to learn.",
    "The opposite: features must carry information about the target.",
    "The test set must never be used for fitting."]],
  ["Why do we check data correlation",
   "Linear regression assumes y changes linearly with x. Pearson's r measures exactly that: r = cov(x, y)/(σₓσᵧ) ranges from −1 to 1, and r ≈ 0 means no linear relationship. Checking first tells you whether a line is a sensible model. If the plot is curved but monotonic, Spearman's correlation may still be high and you might transform the data or use polynomial regression. Correlation is not causation, but no correlation means a linear model will explain little.",
   ["Correct: to confirm a linear relationship is worth modelling.",
    "The learning rate is unrelated to correlation.",
    "You always need a held-out test set.",
    "Regression needs correlation between x and y; it doesn't need uncorrelated data."]],
  ["Which coefficient should you use",
   "Spearman's ρ is Pearson's correlation computed on the ranks of the data. Any strictly increasing relationship (y = eˣ, y = log x) gives ρ = 1 even though it's not linear. Use Pearson for linear relationships and Spearman for monotonic non-linear ones. Neither detects non-monotonic relationships such as a U-shape, so always plot the data too.",
   ["Correct: rank-based, so it captures monotonic trends.",
    "Pearson only measures linear association.",
    "MSE is a loss for regression, not a correlation measure.",
    "Cross-entropy is a classification loss."]],
  ["What is the ordinary least squares (OLS)",
   "Write the model for all N samples as AW = B, where each row of A is [xᵢ, 1] and W = [m, c]ᵀ. The squared error is E = ‖AW − B‖². Setting ∂E/∂W = 0 gives the normal equations AᵀAW = AᵀB, hence W = (AᵀA)⁻¹AᵀB. (AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀ is the Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse. The same formula works for multiple variables (more columns in A) and for polynomial features.",
   ["Correct: the normal-equation solution.",
    "A is usually not square, so it has no inverse; the transpose placement is also wrong.",
    "Wrong matrix order: AAᵀ is N×N, not the parameter-sized matrix.",
    "Adding matrices of different shapes makes no sense here."]],
  ["For the model y = mx + c, what are the parameters",
   "Parameters are the numbers the training procedure adjusts: slope m and intercept c. x is the input and y the output; they're data, not parameters. We choose m and c to minimise the residuals (ground truth minus prediction), usually the sum of squared residuals. The lecture shows three ways: the closed-form OLS solution, grid search over (m, c), and gradient descent (from scratch and with PyTorch).",
   ["Correct: m and c, fitted by minimising the error.",
    "x and y are data, and the test set is never used for fitting.",
    "Learning rate and epochs are optimiser hyperparameters.",
    "Polynomial order is a hyperparameter of polynomial regression."]],
  ["Which is NOT one of the four main assumptions",
   "The four classic assumptions are about the relationship and the residuals: (1) Linearity: E[y|x] is linear in x. (2) Independence: residuals are independent of each other (no autocorrelation, as can happen with time series). (3) Homoscedasticity: residual variance is constant across x. (4) Normality: residuals are normally distributed around 0. Nothing is assumed about the distribution of x itself, so 'uniform inputs' is the odd one out.",
   ["Correct (the false assumption): no distribution is required for the inputs.",
    "Linearity is assumption 1.",
    "Independence of residuals is assumption 2.",
    "Homoscedasticity is assumption 3."]],
  ["What does homoscedasticity mean",
   "Homo = same, scedastic = scatter. In a residual plot (residual vs x or vs prediction), the cloud should be a band of constant width. If it fans out (e.g. errors grow with house price), the data is heteroscedastic: standard errors and confidence intervals are wrong and the fit is dominated by the high-variance region. Fixes include transforming y (e.g. log) or weighted least squares.",
   ["Correct: constant residual variance.",
    "The residuals should be normal (that's assumption 4), not uniform.",
    "Feature scaling is a separate preprocessing step.",
    "Nothing requires a positive output."]],
  ["How can polynomial regression (e.g.",
   "'Linear' in linear least squares refers to the parameters, not the input. y = w₀ + w₁x + w₂x² is a weighted sum of fixed basis functions (1, x, x²). Build A with one column per basis function: row i = [1, xᵢ, xᵢ²]. Then AW = B and W = (AᵀA)⁻¹AᵀB exactly as before. This is the general trick behind basis-function regression.",
   ["Correct: polynomial features plus the same OLS formula.",
    "Gradient descent is possible but not required; OLS works.",
    "Log-transforming and using trees is a different, unrelated method.",
    "k-means does clustering, not coefficient fitting."]],
  ["What is the difference between a linear regression model and a 1st",
   "A polynomial of order N is y = Σₖ wₖxᵏ for k = 0…N. For N = 1 that's y = w₀ + w₁x, which is the straight line y = c + mx. Linear regression is the 1st-order special case of polynomial regression. Exams sometimes test this to check you're not confused by the different names.",
   ["Correct: identical models.",
    "The x² term only appears at order 2.",
    "Linear regression does have an intercept c.",
    "Both can be solved with OLS."]],
  ["What is the hyperparameter of a polynomial",
   "Hyperparameters are chosen before training and control model complexity; parameters are learned during training. For polynomial regression the order N decides how flexible the curve is. Too low underfits; too high overfits, wiggling through every training point. Select N by comparing error on validation data (or with k-fold cross-validation), then report the final performance on the test set.",
   ["Correct: the polynomial order.",
    "The coefficients are parameters, learned by OLS.",
    "Residuals are errors computed after fitting.",
    "Ground-truth values are data."]],
  ["How can overfitting be identified",
   "An overfitted model has learned the noise in the training data rather than the underlying pattern. Its signature is a gap: training error is small (it may even be zero for a high-order polynomial) while error on held-out data is large. Plotting both errors as model complexity grows gives a U-shaped validation curve; the bottom of the U is the best complexity. High error on both sets is underfitting instead.",
   ["Correct: low training error, high validation error.",
    "That's underfitting: the model is too simple.",
    "Training error normally decreases during training.",
    "Validation lower than training is unusual (e.g. dropout effects); it isn't the overfitting signature."]],
  ["In best-practice experimental design",
   "Three roles for data. Training data fits the parameters. Validation data selects hyperparameters (polynomial order, K, learning rate, architecture) and is used for early stopping. Test data is kept secret until the end to estimate real-world performance once. If you tune on test data, the reported number becomes optimistic, because you have implicitly fitted to it. Ideally the test set even comes from a different source (another hospital or scanner) to measure generalisation.",
   ["Correct: validation data selects hyperparameters.",
    "Reporting final performance is the test set's job.",
    "Fitting parameters is the training set's job.",
    "Validation is essential to avoid tuning on the test set."]],
  ["Gradient descent on the krill data",
   "When one feature is ~0.05 and the other ~30, the loss surface is a very elongated valley. The gradient points mostly along the large-scale direction, so updates zig-zag and crawl along the small-scale one. A learning rate small enough to be stable for one direction is far too small for the other. Normalising (subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation) makes the valley round, so the gradient points at the minimum and convergence is fast.",
   ["Correct: normalisation equalises the scales.",
    "Model order doesn't fix the conditioning problem.",
    "Never train on the test set.",
    "Removing the bias changes the model and doesn't fix the scale issue."]],
  ["How does grid search find",
   "Grid search discretises each parameter into a list of values, evaluates the loss for every combination, and keeps the best. It's simple, embarrassingly parallel, and lets you visualise the loss surface, but the cost grows exponentially: k values for each of p parameters means kᵖ evaluations. It suits two or three parameters (or hyperparameters) but not millions of neural network weights, which is why we need gradient descent.",
   ["Correct: exhaustive evaluation on a grid.",
    "That's gradient descent.",
    "That's the analytical OLS solution.",
    "That's dropout."]],
  ["Which sequence describes the K Nearest",
   "kNN is a 'lazy' learner: training just stores the (normalised) data. To classify a new point, compute its distance (usually Euclidean) to every training point, take the K closest, and use the majority class (or average, for regression). Normalisation is step 1 because otherwise the feature with the largest numeric range dominates the distance. Don't confuse kNN (supervised, K = number of neighbours) with k-means (unsupervised, K = number of clusters).",
   ["Correct: the kNN procedure.",
    "That's k-means clustering.",
    "That's a linear model with softmax.",
    "That's decision tree induction."]],
  ["Which is a limitation of KNN",
   "Because kNN keeps the whole training set and compares every query against it, prediction costs O(N·d) time and O(N·d) memory. In high dimension, distances become less informative (curse of dimensionality). It's also sensitive to feature scaling and irrelevant features. Its advantages: it's easy to interpret (you can show the neighbours), has no training phase, and works for both classification and regression.",
   ["Correct: slow and memory-heavy at prediction time.",
    "kNN is actually easy to interpret: you can show the neighbours.",
    "kNN has no gradient-based training.",
    "kNN works for both classification and regression."]],
  ["How should the value of K in KNN",
   "K controls the bias-variance trade-off. K = 1 follows every noisy point (high variance, overfitting), while a very large K smooths everything towards the majority class (high bias). Pick K by evaluating a range of values on validation data, or with k-fold cross-validation, and choose the best. An odd K avoids ties in binary problems. Choosing K on the test set would leak test information.",
   ["Correct: choose K by validation performance.",
    "K = 1 is very sensitive to noise.",
    "Choosing on the test set inflates the reported performance.",
    "There's no reason K should equal the number of features."]],
  ["How can KNN be used for a regression",
   "For regression, the K neighbours' target values are averaged, optionally weighted by 1/distance so closer neighbours count more. The lecture's example estimates a loan amount from similar applicants. The prediction is a piecewise-constant function of the input. It's simple and non-parametric, but it can't extrapolate beyond the range of the training targets.",
   ["Correct: average the neighbours' targets.",
    "kNN works for regression.",
    "Majority voting is for classification.",
    "A decision tree is a different model."]],
  ["Which is an advantage of decision trees",
   "A decision tree is a sequence of axis-aligned questions (income < $35,000? LVR > 80%?), each splitting the data to make the groups purer. You can read the path for any prediction, which makes trees one of the few truly explainable models and valuable in regulated domains like lending. The downsides: they overfit when grown deep, they produce blocky axis-aligned boundaries, and small data changes can change the tree a lot.",
   ["Correct: interpretable rule-based decisions.",
    "Deep trees overfit easily.",
    "Boundaries are axis-aligned and step-shaped, not smooth.",
    "Trees are learned from training data."]],
  ["How can overfitting in decision trees",
   "Pre-pruning stops growth early using limits: maximum depth, minimum samples per split or leaf, or minimum impurity decrease. Post-pruning grows a full tree and then removes subtrees whose removal doesn't hurt validation accuracy (e.g. cost-complexity pruning). Both trade a little training accuracy for better generalisation. Ensembles such as random forests are another remedy.",
   ["Correct: pre-pruning or post-pruning.",
    "One sample per leaf is maximal overfitting.",
    "Trees have no learning rate.",
    "More features per split increases overfitting risk."]],
  ["What is the difference between a decision tree and a random forest",
   "A random forest trains many trees, each on a bootstrap sample of the data and considering a random subset of features at each split, so the trees make different errors. Their votes (or averages) are combined. Averaging de-correlated, high-variance models reduces variance a lot while keeping low bias. You lose some interpretability compared with one tree but gain accuracy and robustness. It's the 'several are better than one' slide.",
   ["Correct: an ensemble of randomised trees with voting.",
    "A forest is many trees, not one deep tree.",
    "There are no neural networks inside.",
    "Ensembling is the key difference."]],
  ["What does a confusion matrix show",
   "To build one, run the model on labelled data and, for each sample, add 1 to the cell (true class, predicted class). Rows are usually ground truth and columns predictions. The diagonal is correct predictions; each off-diagonal cell is a specific kind of mistake (e.g. 'warm' predicted as 'cold'). From the matrix you derive accuracy, and, by focusing on one class, TP/FP/FN/TN, precision, recall and specificity.",
   ["Correct: counts per (true, predicted) pair.",
    "That's a training curve.",
    "That's a correlation matrix.",
    "Weights are model parameters."]],
  ["Simpson's paradox is listed",
   "Example: within each of two hospitals a treatment improves outcomes, but pooled together it looks harmful, because one hospital treats more severe cases and uses the treatment more. A hidden (confounding) variable flips the trend. For linear regression this means a single fit on aggregated data can show the opposite relationship to every subgroup. Check subgroups and include confounders as variables.",
   ["Correct: trends can reverse when groups are combined.",
    "That's an optimisation issue.",
    "That's a violation of the normality assumption.",
    "That's an under-determined system (p > n)."]],
  ["Consider the statements:\nA/ A 1st-order",
   "A: a 1st-order polynomial is y = w₀ + w₁x, the same as linear regression, so true. B: polynomial regression is linear in its parameters, so OLS applies, so false. C: the order is a hyperparameter chosen on validation data, so true. D: coefficients are fitted on training data, and the test set is only for final evaluation, so false. Answer: A and C.",
   ["Correct: A and C are true.",
    "B is false (OLS works for polynomials).",
    "B and D are both false.",
    "D is false (never fit on the test set)."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 3 ----------
  ["For a linear classifier with score",
   "z = w·x + b defines a hyperplane z = 0 (a line in 2D). Any vector lying in the boundary satisfies w·v = 0, so w is the normal vector, perpendicular to the boundary. Changing w rotates (and rescales) the boundary; changing b shifts it parallel to itself. Points with z > 0 are one class and z < 0 the other. In 2D, for ax + by + c = 0, (a, b) is perpendicular to the line.",
   ["Correct: the boundary is z = 0 with w normal to it.",
    "The boundary is z = 0, and w is perpendicular to it, not parallel.",
    "The loss minimum is about training, not the boundary definition.",
    "b shifts the line away from the origin."]],
  ["How is the class score",
   "The signed distance from x to the hyperplane is (w·x + b)/‖w‖, so the score z is that distance scaled by ‖w‖. Its sign tells the side of the boundary (the class) and its magnitude tells how far, often read as confidence. Geometrically a neuron is a projection of x onto w. That's also why points far from the boundary get extreme scores, even if they're unlike any training data (see Week 5, novel classes).",
   ["Correct: a scaled signed distance to the boundary.",
    "Misclassification counts are a metric, not the score.",
    "Raw scores are unbounded; only after a sigmoid or softmax are they in [0, 1].",
    "The score is computed directly from w and b."]],
  ["What is the purpose of a loss function",
   "Training needs a single scalar that says how bad the current parameters are, so an optimiser can improve them. The loss compares predictions with targets over the training data (e.g. average cross-entropy). It must be differentiable (or nearly) for gradient descent. Choosing the loss defines what 'good' means: MSE penalises large errors heavily, hinge loss cares only about the margin, cross-entropy about probabilities.",
   ["Correct: a scalar measure of fit that we minimise.",
    "Initialisation is a separate step.",
    "Architecture choice is a design decision.",
    "Normalisation is preprocessing."]],
  ["Which loss functions are most commonly",
   "Regression outputs a number, so we penalise squared distance: MSE = mean((y − ŷ)²). That corresponds to assuming Gaussian noise and doing maximum likelihood. Classification outputs class probabilities via softmax, so we use cross-entropy = −log p(correct class), which is maximum likelihood under a categorical model. Hinge (multiclass SVM) loss is the margin-based alternative for classification. Accuracy isn't differentiable, so it can't be the training loss.",
   ["Correct: CE for classification, MSE for regression.",
    "Swapped and incorrect pairing.",
    "Accuracy and R² are evaluation metrics, not differentiable training losses.",
    "KL divergence is used for distributions (e.g. VAE), not as the default for both."]],
  ["What is a key difference between hinge",
   "Hinge loss: L = Σ over j ≠ correct of max(0, sⱼ − s_correct + Δ). Once the correct score beats every other score by margin Δ, the loss is exactly 0 and that sample stops influencing training. Cross-entropy: L = −log(softmax(s)_correct). It's never exactly 0, so it keeps pushing the correct probability towards 1 and keeps rewarding larger margins. Softmax plus CE also gives probabilistic outputs; hinge gives only scores.",
   ["Correct: hinge saturates at the margin; CE never fully saturates.",
    "CE is a classification loss.",
    "Hinge works directly on raw scores, while CE needs softmax.",
    "They behave differently, as explained."]],
  ["What does the softmax function do",
   "softmax(s)ᵢ = exp(sᵢ)/Σⱼ exp(sⱼ). Exponentiation makes every value positive and amplifies differences; dividing by the sum makes the outputs a distribution summing to 1. It's a smooth, differentiable approximation of argmax: the largest score gets most of the mass. Combined with cross-entropy it gives the standard classification head. Note that it always distributes all the probability among known classes.",
   ["Correct: scores become a probability distribution.",
    "That's ReLU.",
    "Softmax doesn't select weights.",
    "Gradients come from backpropagation."]],
  ["How is the gradient of the loss used",
   "The gradient ∇L(w) points in the direction of steepest increase of the loss. Moving a small amount against it decreases the loss: w ← w − η∇L(w), where η is the learning rate (step size). Repeat until convergence. With a convex loss (linear regression) this reaches the global minimum; neural network losses are non-convex, but in practice gradient descent finds good minima.",
   ["Correct: step against the gradient.",
    "Moving along the gradient increases the loss.",
    "Inputs are fixed; parameters are updated.",
    "The gradient sets the update direction; it doesn't replace the parameters."]],
  ["What distinguishes gradient descent, stochastic",
   "Batch GD computes the exact gradient over all N samples per update: accurate but expensive, and the data may not fit in memory. SGD uses one random sample per update: cheap and noisy, and the noise can help escape poor minima, but it fluctuates. Mini-batch GD averages over B samples (e.g. 32-256): a good gradient estimate that uses GPU parallelism efficiently. It's what 'SGD' usually means in practice.",
   ["Correct: full data vs one sample vs mini-batch.",
    "Definitions swapped.",
    "They differ in data per update, not activation.",
    "All variants have a learning rate."]],
  ["What does adding momentum",
   "Momentum keeps a velocity v ← βv + ∇L and updates w ← w − ηv. It's an exponential moving average of gradients. In a narrow canyon the gradient flips sign across the walls, so those components cancel, while the consistent component along the valley floor accumulates, so it speeds up. Like a heavy ball rolling, it can also roll over small bumps (shallow local minima). β ≈ 0.9 is typical.",
   ["Correct: smoothing and acceleration.",
    "Nothing guarantees the global minimum on non-convex losses.",
    "Momentum modifies how the loss gradient is used; it still needs a loss.",
    "That's a learning-rate schedule."]],
  ["What is the main difference between plain SGD and Adam",
   "Adam keeps two EMAs per parameter: m (first moment, the mean of gradients: momentum, β₁ ≈ 0.9) and v (second moment, the mean of squared gradients, β₂ ≈ 0.999). The update is w ← w − η·m̂/(√v̂ + ε), with bias-corrected m̂ and v̂. Dividing by √v gives each parameter its own effective step size: large where gradients are small and steady, small where they're large or noisy. Plain SGD uses one global learning rate. Adam inherits RMSProp's fix for AdaGrad's shrinking steps.",
   ["Correct: momentum plus adaptive per-parameter scaling.",
    "Adam is gradient-based.",
    "That's plain SGD.",
    "Adam is the default optimiser for deep networks."]],
  ["Which definition is correct",
   "Epoch: one complete pass over the training set. Batch size: the number of samples used to compute one gradient update. Iteration (step): one update. With N = 10,000 samples and batch size 100, one epoch is 100 iterations. The learning rate is the step size multiplier η. Training typically runs many epochs, and validation loss per epoch is monitored for early stopping.",
   ["Correct: one full pass over the training data.",
    "Batch size is samples per update, not layers.",
    "The learning rate is the step size, not a count.",
    "One update is an iteration, not an epoch."]],
  ["What happens when the learning rate is too small",
   "η scales each step. Too small: progress is tiny, training needs very many epochs, and it can get stuck on plateaus. Too large: steps jump over the minimum, the loss oscillates or explodes to NaN. A common recipe: start relatively large to approach the solution quickly, then decay it (schedules, warm restarts) to settle precisely. Adaptive optimisers such as Adam reduce the sensitivity but don't remove it.",
   ["Correct: slow vs overshooting or divergence.",
    "A small learning rate makes training slow, not overfitting.",
    "The learning rate is one of the most important hyperparameters.",
    "Too large often diverges."]],
  ["What is the difference between a linear classifier and an artificial neuron",
   "A neuron computes a = f(w·x + b). The w·x + b part is exactly a linear classifier (a hyperplane), and the activation f (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU) adds a non-linearity that gives a smooth transition across the boundary. One neuron still gives one boundary; the power comes from stacking many neurons in layers, which requires the non-linearity so the layers don't collapse into one linear map.",
   ["Correct: linear score plus activation.",
    "Neurons are defined by their weights.",
    "Both are trained with gradients.",
    "A neuron contains a linear classifier."]],
  ["What happens to a multi-layer neural network with no activation",
   "Without activations, layer outputs are W₂(W₁x + b₁) + b₂ = (W₂W₁)x + (W₂b₁ + b₂) = Wx + b. Any depth of purely linear layers equals a single linear layer, so boundaries stay hyperplanes and XOR-like problems can't be solved. Activations make each layer a non-linear geometric transformation (bend, fold, squash) that can make classes linearly separable for later layers.",
   ["Correct: it collapses to a single linear map.",
    "Universal approximation needs non-linear activations.",
    "It outputs a linear function, not zeros.",
    "It can represent only linear functions."]],
  ["Which three are common activation",
   "Sigmoid σ(x) = 1/(1+e⁻ˣ) squashes to (0, 1) and saturates at both ends. Tanh squashes to (−1, 1), is zero-centred, and also saturates. ReLU, max(0, x), is cheap, doesn't saturate for x > 0 (it helps with vanishing gradients), and is the default in deep nets. Variants include Leaky ReLU, GELU and SiLU. Softmax is technically an output activation, but MSE and cross-entropy are losses.",
   ["Correct: ReLU, sigmoid and tanh.",
    "Softmax is an output normaliser; MSE and CE are losses.",
    "These are optimisers.",
    "These are layer types for regularisation, normalisation and downsampling."]],
  ["Why can sigmoid activations cause",
   "σ'(x) = σ(x)(1 − σ(x)) has a maximum of 0.25 at x = 0 and approaches 0 for large |x| (saturation). Backpropagation multiplies one such derivative per layer, so with 10 layers the factor can be ≤ 0.25¹⁰ ≈ 10⁻⁶. Early layers receive almost no gradient and stop learning. Remedies: ReLU-type activations, careful initialisation, normalisation layers (batch norm), and residual connections (ResNet).",
   ["Correct: saturation gives tiny derivatives that multiply through layers.",
    "Its derivative is at most 0.25.",
    "Sigmoid outputs are in (0, 1), always positive.",
    "Sigmoid is smooth and differentiable everywhere."]],
  ["What is the difference between machine learning and deep learning",
   "Deep learning is ML with deep neural networks, meaning several hidden layers stacked to learn hierarchical features automatically (edges → parts → objects). Classic ML (linear models, kNN, trees, SVMs) often relies on hand-engineered features. DL can be supervised, unsupervised or self-supervised, and depth, not width, is the defining property.",
   ["Correct: a subset of ML with multiple hidden layers.",
    "DL is used with all learning paradigms, including self-supervised LLMs.",
    "'Deep' refers to depth, not width.",
    "DL is a subset of ML, so there is a difference."]],
  ["What is backpropagation?",
   "The loss depends on early weights through every later layer. The chain rule gives ∂L/∂W₁ = ∂L/∂a_L · ∂a_L/∂a_(L−1) · … · ∂a₁/∂W₁. Backpropagation computes these products efficiently: the forward pass stores the activations, then the backward pass reuses the gradients from later layers to compute earlier ones, at roughly the cost of one forward pass. PyTorch autograd does this automatically with loss.backward().",
   ["Correct: the chain rule applied layer by layer, backwards.",
    "That's the forward pass.",
    "Initialisation happens before training.",
    "Augmentation modifies data, not gradients."]],
  ["In neural network terminology, what are network depth",
   "Depth is how many layers of transformation are stacked; width is how many units each layer has. Both increase capacity. One wide hidden layer is already a universal approximator, but deeper networks can represent many functions with far fewer parameters by composing features hierarchically. Deeper networks are harder to optimise (vanishing gradients), which is why ResNets and normalisation matter.",
   ["Correct: depth = layers, width = units per layer.",
    "Swapped.",
    "Batch size and learning rate are training hyperparameters.",
    "Epochs and classes are unrelated to architecture shape."]],
  ["Which set of techniques helps mitigate",
   "Identify overfitting by plotting training and validation loss per epoch: when validation loss starts rising while training loss keeps falling, the model is memorising. Early stopping keeps the weights from the best validation epoch. Regularisation (an L2 weight-decay penalty) discourages large weights. Dropout randomly zeroes activations so the network can't rely on any single unit. Data augmentation and more data also help.",
   ["Correct: regularisation, dropout and early stopping.",
    "That increases overfitting.",
    "Without validation you can't detect overfitting.",
    "Tuning on the test set biases the final estimate."]],
  ["Why is an exponential moving average",
   "A centred moving average over a window of 2N+1 samples must store all those values. EMA uses a recursion: yₜ = αyₜ₋₁ + (1 − α)xₜ. Only the previous output is stored, yet it effectively averages the whole past with exponentially decaying weights (αᵏ for a lag of k). α close to 1 means long memory and strong smoothing. This filter is the core of momentum, Adam's moment estimates, batch norm running statistics, and EMA teacher networks.",
   ["Correct: one stored value, recursive update.",
    "α is a hyperparameter.",
    "Weights decay exponentially, not equally.",
    "It works on any signal, including gradients."]],
  ["What is the relationship between the last layer",
   "Think of a deep network as a feature extractor followed by a classifier. All hidden layers transform the input geometrically so that, in the final feature space, classes are (nearly) linearly separable. The last layer, a linear map plus softmax, is then just a linear classifier on those features. That's why transfer learning can replace only the last layer for a new task while reusing the features.",
   ["Correct: a linear classifier on learned features.",
    "The last layer of a classifier is usually fully connected.",
    "No clustering is involved.",
    "They're the same mechanism."]],
  ["How are the weights W and b",
   "If all weights started equal, every neuron in a layer would compute the same output and get the same gradient, so they'd stay identical forever (the symmetry problem). Random initialisation from a uniform or normal distribution breaks the symmetry. The scale matters: too large saturates activations or explodes; too small vanishes. Schemes like Xavier/Glorot and He/Kaiming set the variance based on the number of inputs. Biases are often initialised to 0.",
   ["Correct: small random values.",
    "All zeros causes the symmetry problem.",
    "All ones also causes the symmetry problem and saturation.",
    "Weights aren't copied from data."]],
  ["Which of the following statements about Artificial Neural Networks",
   "Go through each option. A: non-linear activations (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh) let ANNs model non-linear relationships, so false. B: a neuron first computes z = W·x + b, then a = σ(z), so the activation comes after the weighted sum, so false. C: backpropagation uses the chain rule to get gradients, and optimisers (SGD, Adam, RMSProp) update the weights to minimise the loss, so true. D: deeper isn't always better; it can overfit, suffer vanishing or exploding gradients, and cost more, so false.",
   ["False: activations make ANNs non-linear.",
    "False: the activation is applied after the weighted sum.",
    "Correct: the chain rule plus an optimiser such as SGD.",
    "False: depth must match the data and task."]],
  ["How many learnable parameters does an MLP with 2 inputs",
   "A fully connected layer with n_in inputs and n_out outputs has n_in·n_out weights plus n_out biases. Hidden layer: 2·10 + 10 = 30. Output layer: 10·1 + 1 = 11. Total = 41. Exam tip: write each layer as (n_in + 1)·n_out, here (2+1)·10 + (10+1)·1 = 30 + 11.",
   ["Correct: 30 + 11 = 41.",
    "Only the hidden layer (forgets the output layer).",
    "Hidden weights only (forgets biases and the output layer).",
    "Forgets the output layer's weights or biases."]],

  // ---------- WEEK 4 ----------
  ["What is the difference between image classification, semantic",
   "Classification assumes one dominant object per image and outputs one label (99% cat). Semantic segmentation assumes no notion of objects, only pixels: every pixel gets a class, but two adjacent cats merge into one 'cat' region. Object detection assumes 0 or more objects and outputs a class plus a bounding box for each. Instance segmentation combines both: per-object, per-pixel masks that separate the two cats.",
   ["Correct: one label vs per-pixel vs boxes.",
    "Classification and segmentation definitions swapped.",
    "Only classification gives one label per image.",
    "Detection gives boxes; segmentation gives per-pixel classes."]],
  ["Which is a major assumption made when using datasets",
   "Benchmarks like ImageNet drove progress, but a benchmark score assumes (1) the dataset distribution matches the application's data, and (2) the ground truth is unambiguous for every image, object or pixel. In practice deployment images differ (lighting, devices, populations), and many labels are ambiguous (is it a dog or a wolf? where exactly is the tumour boundary?). So an 87% benchmark accuracy may not mean 87% in the real world.",
   ["Correct: the dataset matches deployment data.",
    "Test images must be unseen, not identical.",
    "Most images contain one or a few classes.",
    "Labels must be meaningful for learning."]],
  ["Why is a convolutional layer more useful",
   "A fully connected layer on a 224×224×3 image needs 150,528 weights per neuron and treats pixels as an unordered list: shuffling all pixels the same way gives identical results. A convolution slides a small filter (e.g. 3×3×3 = 27 weights) over the image. Local connectivity exploits the fact that neighbouring pixels are correlated. Weight sharing means one filter detects its pattern (a 'nose') anywhere, with far fewer parameters and better generalisation.",
   ["Correct: local structure plus weight sharing.",
    "Conv layers have far fewer parameters.",
    "That's what FC layers do; convolution respects order.",
    "All layers need training."]],
  ["What type of invariance",
   "Shifting the input shifts the activation map by the same amount (translation equivariance). After pooling or global aggregation the output becomes approximately translation invariant: a cat in the corner or the centre is still detected. Convolutions are not invariant to rotation or scale, because a filter tuned to an upright nose of a certain size won't match a rotated or much larger one. Data augmentation (rotations, scaling) and multiscale architectures compensate.",
   ["Correct: translation invariance.",
    "Rotation must be learned, e.g. through augmentation.",
    "Scale must be learned or handled with multiscale designs.",
    "Colour changes alter the filter responses."]],
  ["Consider a batch of 100 feature tensors",
   "Output size: (N − F + 2P)/S + 1 = (32 − 3 + 2)/1 + 1 = 32, so spatial size is kept. The batch stays 100 and there are 5 filters, so 5 output channels: [100, 5, 32, 32], so A is true and B is false. Parameters: each filter spans all 3 input channels, 3·3·3 = 27 weights + 1 bias = 28, and 5 filters give 140. So C (20) is false and D is true. The batch size never affects parameter count.",
   ["A is true but C is false (20 is wrong).",
    "Correct: A and D.",
    "B is false (5 channels), and C is false.",
    "B is false (the output has 5 channels)."]],
  ["You are designing a CNN for image classification",
   "Track [C, H, W] step by step. Input [3, 64, 64]. Conv 16@3×3, P=1, S=1: (64 − 3 + 2)/1 + 1 = 64, so [16, 64, 64]. ReLU doesn't change the shape. MaxPool 2×2 S2 gives [16, 32, 32]. Conv 32@3×3, P=1: [32, 32, 32]. ReLU keeps it. MaxPool gives [32, 16, 16]. Rule of thumb: '3×3 with padding 1, stride 1' keeps size, each 2×2/2 pool halves it, and channels always equal the number of filters in the last conv.",
   ["Wrong channel count (16 is from the first conv).",
    "Correct: [32, 16, 16].",
    "Size before the second pooling.",
    "One pooling too many."]],
  ["What is the output spatial size of a convolution",
   "Pad the input by P on each side, giving length N + 2P. A filter of size F has N + 2P − F + 1 valid positions at stride 1, and with stride S we take every S-th position: (N − F + 2P)/S + 1. Worked examples: 7×7, F=3, P=0, S=1 gives 5×5; S=2 gives 3×3. With F=3 and P=1 at S=1 the size is preserved ('same' padding). Memorise this formula; it appears in most CNN exam questions.",
   ["Correct: (N − F + 2P)/S + 1.",
    "Not a valid size formula.",
    "Not a valid size formula.",
    "Not a valid size formula."]],
  ["A 7×7 input is convolved",
   "(7 − 3 + 0)/3 + 1 = 4/3 + 1 ≈ 2.33. A non-integer means the filter positions don't tile the input evenly: the last stride would run off the edge. Frameworks like PyTorch silently floor it (giving 2×2 and ignoring the remaining pixels), but by design you should choose N, F, P and S so the result is an integer, for example by adding zero padding.",
   ["Correct: not an integer, so it doesn't fit evenly.",
    "3×3 is the result for stride 2.",
    "5×5 is the result for stride 1.",
    "7×7 would need padding 1 and stride 1."]],
  ["A conv layer applies 6 filters",
   "Each filter must cover all input channels: 5 × 5 × 3 = 75 weights, plus 1 bias = 76 parameters per filter. Six filters: 6 × 76 = 456. The output is 6 activation maps of size (32 − 5)/1 + 1 = 28, i.e. 28×28×6 with no padding. The parameter count is independent of the image size (32×32), which is the payoff of weight sharing.",
   ["Correct: (5·5·3 + 1) × 6 = 456.",
    "Forgets the 3 input channels and the biases: 5·5·6.",
    "Forgets the biases: 75 × 6.",
    "That's an output-size-style number, not parameters."]],
  ["A batch of 100 RGB images",
   "PyTorch convolution layers expect tensors in N×C×H×W order (batch, channels, height, width). So 100 RGB images of 224×224 are [100, 3, 224, 224]. Libraries like PIL or matplotlib use H×W×C for a single image, which is why transforms such as ToTensor() permute the axes. Getting this order wrong is a common source of shape errors.",
   ["Correct: B×C×H×W.",
    "That's channels-last with the batch at the end.",
    "Batch must come first.",
    "Missing a spatial dimension."]],
  ["Why are pooling layers used",
   "Max pooling takes the maximum in each window (e.g. 2×2 with stride 2), halving H and W, so it keeps the strongest activations and adds a little translation robustness. Average pooling takes the mean. Pooling reduces computation and memory and enlarges the receptive field of later layers. It has hyperparameters (type, window size, stride) but no learnable parameters. A strided convolution is a learnable alternative for downsampling.",
   ["Correct: downsampling with no parameters.",
    "ReLU adds non-linearity; pooling has no weights.",
    "That's batch norm.",
    "Pooling reduces resolution (upsampling is transpose convolution)."]],
  ["What do convolutional filters typically learn",
   "Visualising trained CNNs shows a hierarchy. First-layer filters resemble edge and colour-contrast detectors (Gabor-like). Middle layers respond to textures and simple parts (eyes, wheels). Deep layers respond to whole object parts or objects, because each layer's receptive field covers a larger area of the input. Deep Dream and feature visualisation optimise an input image to maximise a filter, revealing what it 'looks for'.",
   ["Correct: simple to complex with depth.",
    "Reversed.",
    "Filters specialise differently.",
    "They learn spatial patterns, not just colours."]],
  ["Why is generalisation in computer vision",
   "The same object can produce wildly different pixel arrays: viewpoint, scale, deformation (a cat curled vs stretched), occlusion, illumination, background clutter, and variation within a class (chair styles). Meanwhile different classes can look similar. A model generalises when the features it learned on training images (e.g. 1.2M ImageNet images) remain discriminative on new images with all this variability. Augmentation, large diverse datasets and regularisation help.",
   ["Correct: large appearance variability.",
    "Images have many pixels.",
    "CNNs are trained with gradient descent.",
    "Many images share a label."]],
  ["What is the difference between dropout and batch normalisation",
   "Dropout(p): during training each activation is zeroed with probability p (the rest are scaled by 1/(1−p)), which prevents co-adaptation, a bit like training an ensemble. At eval it is off. Batch norm: normalise each channel with the batch mean and variance, then apply learnable γ and β. It stabilises and speeds up training and allows higher learning rates. At eval it uses running averages of the statistics. So one regularises and the other normalises.",
   ["Correct: regularisation vs normalisation.",
    "Only dropout removes activations.",
    "Swapped.",
    "Both behave differently in train vs eval mode."]],
  ["What is the role of data augmentation",
   "Augmentation applies random, label-preserving transformations each time an image is loaded: flips, crops, rotations, scaling, colour jitter, blur. The model effectively sees new variations every epoch, which (1) enlarges the training distribution, (2) teaches invariances that convolutions don't have by design (rotation, scale, colour), and (3) reduces overfitting. It's applied to training data only (torchvision.transforms), and the transformations must not change the true label.",
   ["Correct: random transformations for diversity and generalisation.",
    "The test set should stay untouched.",
    "Augmentation doesn't fix labels.",
    "The loss is still required."]],
  ["What is transfer learning and how is it practically",
   "Features learned on a large dataset (edges, textures, parts) are useful for many vision tasks. Practically: (1) load a pre-trained model (e.g. torchvision ResNet on ImageNet), (2) replace the final classification layer to match your number of classes, (3) either freeze the backbone and train only the new head (little data), or fine-tune all layers with a small learning rate (more data). This gives good accuracy with far less labelled data and compute.",
   ["Correct: reuse pre-trained weights, replace the head, freeze or fine-tune.",
    "Never train on the test set.",
    "Transfer learning reuses features, not labels.",
    "k-means is unrelated."]],
  ["What innovation did ResNet",
   "A residual block computes y = F(x) + x: the layers only need to learn a residual correction F, and the identity shortcut passes x (and, backwards, the gradient) straight through. Without it, very deep plain networks trained worse than shallower ones because gradients vanish through many multiplications. With skip connections ResNets reached 50, 101 and 152 layers. Skip connections are now everywhere (DenseNet, UNet, transformers).",
   ["Correct: residual skip connections.",
    "Max pooling existed long before.",
    "Sigmoid makes vanishing gradients worse.",
    "ResNet is convolutional."]],
  ["What is a UNet",
   "UNet (Ronneberger et al., 2015) has a contracting encoder (conv plus pooling: more channels, lower resolution, large receptive field to understand context) and an expanding decoder (upsampling or transpose conv back to full resolution). Skip connections concatenate encoder features at each scale into the decoder, so fine spatial detail lost by pooling is recovered. The output is a per-pixel map, ideal for segmentation (medical CT, MRI). It's also the standard denoiser in diffusion models.",
   ["Correct: a multiscale encoder-decoder with skips.",
    "That's an RNN.",
    "A huge kernel explodes the parameter count.",
    "Not a tree model."]],
  ["Why not simply remove all pooling",
   "Each 3×3 conv (stride 1) grows the receptive field by 2 pixels: after L layers it is 2L + 1. To let every output pixel see the whole 224-pixel image you'd need L ≈ 112 layers, which is hard to backpropagate through and very expensive at full resolution. 11×11 kernels need ~22 layers but have ~13× more weights each. Downsampling grows the receptive field exponentially, and the UNet decoder restores resolution, which solves both problems.",
   ["Correct: too many layers needed for a global receptive field.",
    "Convolutions stack fine.",
    "With padding the size is kept.",
    "Each 3×3 kernel has 9·C_in weights."]],
  ["A batch of 8 tensors",
   "Size: (64 − 5 + 2·2)/1 + 1 = 64, so the output is [8, 32, 64, 64] and A is true, C false (60 would be without padding). Parameters: (5·5·16 + 1)·32 = (400 + 1)·32 = 12,832, so B is true. D (12,800) forgets the 32 biases. Pattern: padding P = (F − 1)/2 keeps the size for odd F at stride 1.",
   ["Correct: A and B.",
    "D forgets the biases.",
    "C would be the no-padding size.",
    "Both C and D are wrong."]],
  ["Input [3,128,128]",
   "Conv: (128 − 3 + 2·1)/2 + 1 = 127/2 + 1 = 64.5, and PyTorch floors it to 64, giving [8, 64, 64] (a 3×3, stride-2, padding-1 conv halves the size). ReLU doesn't change the shape. MaxPool 2×2 S2 gives [8, 32, 32]. Channels = 8 filters. So two halvings take 128 → 64 → 32.",
   ["Correct: [8, 32, 32].",
    "Forgets the pooling step.",
    "Channels become 8, not 3.",
    "One halving too many."]],
  ["The feature map [32,16,16]",
   "Flattening gives 32 × 16 × 16 = 8,192 features. nn.Linear(8192, 10) has a weight matrix of 10 × 8,192 = 81,920 entries plus 10 biases, so 81,930. Notice this one layer has more parameters than many conv layers combined, which is why modern CNNs use global average pooling before the classifier.",
   ["Correct: 81,920 + 10.",
    "Forgets the 10 biases.",
    "Wrong flatten size.",
    "Uses 512 features instead of 8,192."]],
  ["Consider the 4 statements about a MaxPool2d",
   "Max pooling has no learnable parameters: it just takes the maximum in each window, so A is true and D false. It operates on each channel independently, so channels stay 64 and spatial size halves: (32 − 2)/2 + 1 = 16, so the output is [64, 16, 16]. C is true and B false (it doesn't change the channel count).",
   ["Correct: A and C.",
    "B is false (pooling keeps channels).",
    "D is false (no parameters).",
    "Both B and D are false."]]
);
